The drying time is assumed to be proportional to the total amount of clothing that needs to dry.  

- 15 shirts → 1 hour  
- 1 shirt → \( \frac{1\text{ hour}}{15} = \frac{1}{15}\text{ hour} \)

For 35 shirts:

\[
35 \times \frac{1}{15}\text{ hour} = \frac{35}{15}\text{ hour}
= \frac{7}{3}\text{ hour}
= 2\frac{1}{3}\text{ hour}
\]

\[
2\frac{1}{3}\text{ hour} = 2\text{ hours } + \frac{1}{3}\times60\text{ min}
= 2\text{ hours }20\text{ minutes}
\]

**So 35 shirts would take about 2 hours 20 minutes to dry.** (If the shirts could all be spread out in a single layer with equal sun exposure, the time would remain 1 hour; the proportional answer assumes the drying capacity is limited and scales with the number of shirts.)
